60,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"The candidates are A: Home Follow-Up, B: Day Rehab, C: Perinatal Support, D: Chronic Care.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Shion,,"@Jonathan, A aligns with the readmission data. It is the most proven model.","{""words"":13,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, A needs nurses. We have vacancies. But C uses midwifery slack.","{""words"":12,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"@Midori, A avoids the car park and room bottlenecks. B and C will strain the site.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"@Kenji, A is owned by nursing. C is joint. The joint clinic failed. A is safer.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Shizue, A is lean. It doesn't need new fitting out. It fits the reduced budget.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Jonathan, A is the only one with strong outcome data. The others are unproven here.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, I agree. The midwifery slack is good, but the joint aspect is risky.","{""words"":14,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Shion, A is the only one that doesn't add to the physical load. I support it.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Shion, A avoids the joint management trap. It is the most sustainable choice.","{""words"":13,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Midori, the agency cover is a concern, but A is the only one with proven value.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Midori, the booking system issue is real, but we can fix the logging. The model is sound.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, I am convinced. The staffing risk is manageable if we focus on the existing team.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Shion, I am happy to support A. It solves the capacity problem.","{""words"":12,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Shion, I agree. A is the clear winner. It respects our constraints.","{""words"":12,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,I vote for A. It is the only option that fits the budget and the evidence.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Shion,,I vote for A. The data supports it. It is the logical choice.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Midori,,I vote for A. It is the most balanced option for our staffing reality.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Kenji,,I vote for A. It avoids the physical bottlenecks. It is the safest bet.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Shizue,,I vote for A. It avoids the joint management failure. It is the wise choice.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
